From: MacGameVideoFix <https://github.com/MathiasKowoll/MacGameVideoFix>
Subject: [PATCH] winebus.sys: the USB emulation refuses a wired-only audio request on the pad's behalf

WHERE THIS CAME FROM

MacGameVideoFix's own series, mgvf-NNNN, written on 2026-09-08, the sixth of
ours and the fifth in the controller-bus set. It is a follow-up to mgvf-0005
and does not apply without it: it changes that patch's own translation, in the
emulation path and nowhere else. Nothing in it is specific to this project.

THE FAULT

mgvf-0005 tells a client that a DualSense on Bluetooth is on a cable. A client
that believes it asks for what a wired pad has, and the pad's audio hardware --
its speaker, its headphone jack and its microphone -- is the part of it that a
cable is for.

The first trace of the emulation under a real title, emulation-224719.log on
2026-09-08, has the pad presented at 651695.444 ("presenting Bluetooth
DualSense 054c:0df2 as USB 054c:0df2, 405-byte report descriptor") and then two
output reports, which are the only two that title ever wrote through it:

  651734.192  02 0d 17 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 05 ...  (224719:4633)
  651734.193  02 80 40 00 00 00 00 00 0c 00 00 00 ...  (224719:4651)

At 651734.215, 22 milliseconds after the second, IOKit reported the device
removed: the Bluetooth link was gone.

The translation is not what is wrong. Both reports were packed faithfully into
a 0x31 of 78 bytes with the sequence byte, the 0x10 tag and a CRC, and the
trace's own hex dumps show byte 3 onward of each packet equal to byte 1 onward
of the report the client wrote. The first is ordinary: flag0 0x0d is rumble and
the two trigger-effect blocks, and the trigger payload is in the report. The
second asks for nothing but the pad's audio path: flag0 0x80 with the
audio-control byte 0x0c, and every other byte zero.

So what this patch changes is not how the emulation translates but what it lets
through.

THE CHANGE

On the way from a client's USB output report to the Bluetooth one, the audio
fields are taken back out: the validity-flag bits that enable them are cleared
and the bytes those bits govern are zeroed, before the CRC is computed, so what
the pad checks covers what it is actually sent. Every other byte goes out
exactly as the client wrote it -- rumble, both trigger blocks, the lightbar,
the player LEDs, the second flag byte in full -- and a report that asks for no
audio field comes out byte for byte as it went in.

Each bit is cleared only together with the byte it governs, and only when the
client set it. A client that leaves a byte in the audio range set without
setting its bit has written a byte the pad ignores, and it is passed through:
what is refused is the request, not the bytes.

Only in the emulation path. The code is reached from
dualsense_usb_set_output_report, which exists only for a device carrying
HIDRAW_FIXUP_DUALSENSE_USB; a client that knows the pad is on Bluetooth and
writes a 0x31 itself is not this patch's business and its report is untouched.

One line of trace when something is dropped, naming the bits cleared and the
bytes zeroed. TRACE and not WARN, deliberately: a client that sets a volume
every frame would fill a default log with it, and refusing the request is what
the emulation is for rather than something going wrong.

HOW THE LAYOUT WAS ESTABLISHED

Not from memory and not from the community's field names: from the
disassembly of the library that wrote the report. libScePad 1.0.4.1, the copy
two current titles ship, read with llvm-objdump at image base 0x180000000.

  scePadSetVolumeGain, exported at RVA 0x8c30, reaches the report builder at
  0x18000c8e0. For each of three fields of its four-byte argument that is not
  0xff it sets one bit of report byte 1 and writes one byte, translated
  through a 256-entry table of its own:

    0x18000c9e1  or 0x20 into byte 1   ->  0x18000c9f1  write byte 6
    0x18000c9fa  or 0x10 into byte 1   ->  0x18000ca0b  write byte 5
    0x18000ca14  or 0x40 into byte 1   ->  0x18000ca25  write byte 7

  and sends the result at 0x18000ca35 as report 0x02, 0x30 bytes long.

  scePadSetAudioOutPath, exported at RVA 0x8b40, reaches 0x18000c710, which
  sets bit 0x80 of byte 1 at 0x18000c850 and writes byte 8 at 0x18000c857:
  the pad's own audio-control byte with bits 0x30 replaced by the path it was
  given (0x18000c7c4 clears them, 0x18000c7c8 shifts the argument in). A
  second entry point at 0x18000c235 clears bits 0x0c of that same byte,
  writes a two-bit value into them and sends the same report at 0x18000c2cb.
  0x0c is exactly what byte 8 of the report that ended the trace carried.

So the audio group is bits 0x10, 0x20, 0x40 and 0x80 of the first flag byte,
governing bytes 5, 6, 7 and 8 in that order -- three volume or gain bytes and
an audio-control byte -- and nothing else in the report. Those four bits and
those four bytes are what this patch masks, and no more.

The second flag byte is left alone, all of it, and that is a decision with its
own evidence. The report that ended the trace also carried bit 0x40 there, and
that bit is not audio: libScePad sets it on every DualSense output report it
sends, in its own send path at 0x1800072e2, together with byte 37 (written at
0x18000737b) and bit 0x04 of byte 39 (0x18000736d), which are vibration
fields. Nothing on this disk says what the other bits of that byte mean, so
they go through untouched.

The host test beside the descriptors in the build directory, usb_emu_test.c,
asserts all of the above against the captured bytes: the trace's first report
comes out byte for byte as the driver packed it that night, CRC 2d1d855d
included, with nothing dropped; the second comes out with 0x80 reported
dropped, byte 8 zeroed, its second flag byte still 0x40 and the rest of the
packet the trace's own with only that taken out; and a report that asks for all
four fields at once keeps its rumble and trigger halves and loses only the
audio ones.

NOT COVERED

THAT THE REQUEST KILLED THE LINK. It is not established, and the corpus argues
against it. What is measured is the sequence: two writes, then the removal 22
milliseconds after the second. Against that stands the session in
steam-hid-game-145815.log, where the same pad -- 054c:0df2, on Bluetooth,
presented truthfully -- was written 6477 times over about two hours, and where
a client sent the same audio request twice, at 624067.389 (log line 53267:
flag0 0x82, audio-control byte 0x0c, which is the trace's killer packet with
one more bit set) and at 624190.818 (flag0 0x8d). The link stayed up for more
than 900 seconds after each, and the one removal in that log is 1037 seconds
after the first of them. A DualSense that is written that request over
Bluetooth does not necessarily drop the link.

What this patch rests on instead is narrower and does not need the causation:
the request is for hardware the pad has on a cable, the emulation is the reason
a client asks the pad for it in the USB report's shape, and there is nothing
under wine on the other end of the pad's audio path to make the request worth
forwarding. Dropping it costs nothing measured and leaves less of mgvf-0005's
lie reaching the pad. WHAT ACTUALLY ENDED THAT SESSION IS UNKNOWN. The one
thing in that trace that the working session has none of is the feature report
0x08 written at 651734.142, with the Bluetooth CRC mgvf-0005 appends: there are
no feature writes at all in steam-hid-game-145815.log. That is a difference,
not a finding, and it has not been investigated.

Which of bytes 5, 6 and 7 is the headphone volume, which the speaker's and
which the microphone's is NOT asserted. What is established is that libScePad
writes each of them from one field of its volume-gain argument and enables it
with one bit; the pairing of bit to byte is what this patch needs and is what
was read.

Whether a pad that is sent a report with the audio bits cleared behaves
differently from one that is sent no such report at all has not been measured;
nor has any of this, yet, against a live pad -- as with mgvf-0005, the first
use belongs in a +hid trace.

---
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.c
@@ -217,6 +217,76 @@
     tail[1] = (crc >> 8) & 0xff;
     tail[2] = (crc >> 16) & 0xff;
     tail[3] = (crc >> 24) & 0xff;
+}
+
+/* Where a byte of the USB output report lands in the packed Bluetooth one: the
+ * id stays the id and everything after it moves two places, behind the
+ * sequence byte and the 0x10. Used only by the audio masking below, which is
+ * the one thing here that looks at a byte by its meaning rather than copying
+ * the block whole. */
+#define DUALSENSE_BT_OF(usb_byte) ((usb_byte) + 2)
+
+/* MGVF mgvf-0007: take the pad's audio settings back out of the report.
+ *
+ * WHY. The emulation tells a client the pad is on a cable, and a client that
+ * believes that asks for things only a wired pad has. A DualSense's speaker,
+ * headphone jack and microphone hang off its USB audio interfaces; over
+ * Bluetooth a Windows client reaches none of them, and the request is one the
+ * pad can only refuse. Nothing else in the report is affected: the rumble
+ * motors, the trigger effects, the lightbar and the player LEDs are the same
+ * fields on both transports, and they are passed through untouched.
+ *
+ * WHICH BITS, AND HOW THAT WAS ESTABLISHED. From the disassembly of Sony's own
+ * libScePad 1.0.4.1 (llvm-objdump, image base 0x180000000), which is the
+ * library that built the request that started this:
+ *
+ *   scePadSetVolumeGain (export at RVA 0x8c30) reaches the report builder at
+ *   0x18000c8e0. For each of three fields of its four-byte argument that is
+ *   not 0xff it sets one bit of byte 1 and writes one translated byte:
+ *   0x18000c9e1 sets 0x20 and 0x18000c9f1 writes byte 6; 0x18000c9fa sets 0x10
+ *   and 0x18000ca0b writes byte 5; 0x18000ca14 sets 0x40 and 0x18000ca25
+ *   writes byte 7. It then sends the report, id 0x02 and 0x30 bytes long, at
+ *   0x18000ca35.
+ *
+ *   scePadSetAudioOutPath (export at RVA 0x8b40) reaches 0x18000c710, which
+ *   sets bit 0x80 of byte 1 at 0x18000c850 and writes byte 8 at 0x18000c857 --
+ *   the pad's own audio-control byte with bits 0x30 replaced by the path it was
+ *   given (0x18000c7c4 clears them, 0x18000c7c8 shifts the argument into
+ *   place). A second entry point at 0x18000c235 sets bits 0x0c of the same
+ *   byte and sends the same report at 0x18000c2cb, and 0x0c is exactly what
+ *   the report that ended the trace carried in byte 8.
+ *
+ * So the audio group is bits 0x10, 0x20, 0x40 and 0x80 of the first flag byte,
+ * governing bytes 5, 6, 7 and 8 in that order, and nothing else. Each bit is
+ * cleared only with the byte it governs, and only when the client set it: a
+ * report that asks for none of them comes out of here byte for byte as the
+ * client wrote it.
+ *
+ * WHAT IS DELIBERATELY LEFT ALONE. The second flag byte, all of it. The report
+ * that ended the trace also carried bit 0x40 there, and that bit is not audio:
+ * libScePad sets it on every DualSense output report it sends, in its own send
+ * path at 0x1800072e2, together with byte 37 (written at 0x18000737b) and bit
+ * 0x04 of byte 39 (0x18000736d), which are vibration fields. Nothing measured
+ * says what the remaining bits of that byte mean, so they are passed through.
+ *
+ * Returns the bits it cleared, for the caller to trace. */
+static unsigned char dualsense_bt_drop_audio(unsigned char *out, unsigned int in_len)
+{
+    unsigned char dropped = 0, bit;
+    unsigned int i, byte;
+
+    for (i = 0; i < DUALSENSE_USB_OUTPUT_AUDIO_COUNT; i++)
+    {
+        bit = DUALSENSE_USB_OUTPUT_AUDIO_FIRST_FLAG << i;
+        byte = DUALSENSE_USB_OUTPUT_AUDIO_FIRST_BYTE + i;
+        if (byte >= in_len) break; /* the client's report is shorter than this byte */
+        if (!(out[DUALSENSE_BT_OF(DUALSENSE_USB_OUTPUT_FLAG0)] & bit)) continue;
+        out[DUALSENSE_BT_OF(DUALSENSE_USB_OUTPUT_FLAG0)] &= ~bit;
+        out[DUALSENSE_BT_OF(byte)] = 0;
+        dropped |= bit;
+    }
+
+    return dropped;
 }
 
